# Authored DTL owns prose and stable IDs. Regenerate registration with ExportSevenDayRegistration.gd.
return

label Day6
\At the top of the stairs, my right hand rises to knock on the flat door; my left hand holds the closed long black umbrella. #id:line.Day6.one
\Just before my knuckles reach the wood, the door swings inward. #id:line.Day6.002
\Lavinia stands on the threshold in her stockinged feet. Without a word, her hand takes the closed long umbrella from my left hand and sets it inside the hallway stand. #id:line.Day6.003
\My raised right hand stays arrested in the air for half a beat—then redirects two inches to the wooden doorjamb and completes one light, exact knock against the frame. #id:line.Day6.004
\Lavinia watches the knuckles finish the knock against the wood before she steps back to let me cross the threshold. #id:line.Day6.005
\In the narrow kitchen off the hall, the kettle switch clicks down on the first press. #id:line.Day6.006
\No need to look for the light switch; the pale strip under the upper cabinet is already on. #id:line.Day6.007
\Right hand to the second cupboard from the window\: two thin porcelain cups on the lower shelf, the one with the hairline chip turned toward the wall so only my own fingers will take it. Left hand to the tin behind the flour jar\: loose black tea, then the small square honey pot. #id:line.Day6.008
\Beside the sink, on the wire drying rack, sits a third mug—heavy blue stoneware, not part of the flat's set. Three clear drops of water still cling to the inner rim, and the handle is turned to the left. #id:line.Day6.009
\On the threshold behind the counter, Lavinia's stockinged feet come to a stop. #id:line.Day6.010
\In the dark glass of the kitchen window above the sink, her reflection leans one shoulder against the doorframe, arms folded over her cardigan, watching my hands at the counter. #id:line.Day6.011
\“You still remember where the honey is,” she says. #id:line.Day6.012
\“It hasn't moved.” #id:line.Day6.013
\“The cups moved.” #id:line.Day6.014
\“Only to the left side of the shelf.” #id:line.Day6.015
\My fingers lift the heavy blue stoneware mug from the wire rack, dry its base once with the dish towel, and place it on the highest shelf above the refrigerator—behind the glass salad bowl, out of sight from the doorway. #id:line.Day6.016
\In the window glass above the sink, the corner of Lavinia's mouth curves. #id:line.Day6.017
\“She brought soup on Wednesday,” Lavinia says calmly. “After the exhibition.” #id:line.Day6.018
\“How thoughtful of her,” I say, opening the tea tin. “She always likes to leave a container she has to come back for.” #id:line.Day6.019
\Lavinia steps out of the doorway and comes up beside my elbow at the counter, close enough that her sleeve brushes mine as I measure one spoonful of loose leaves into the warmed pot. #id:line.Day6.020
\“A little more,” she says. #id:line.Day6.021
\I pause with the spoon above the tin. “You used to say a second spoonful made it bitter.” #id:line.Day6.022
\“I tried it your way,” she says, looking down into the dark leaves. “I like it stronger now.” #id:line.Day6.023
\Half a spoonful more drops into the pot. #id:line.Day6.024
\“Like that?” #id:line.Day6.025
\“Yes.” #id:line.Day6.026
\Boiling water fills the porcelain pot, and we carry the tray into the living room. #id:line.Day6.027
\As I set the tray on the low table, my hand goes to the wall switch beside the bookcase and clicks off the overhead bulb—leaving only the low amber shade in the corner and the pale blue square of the television across the rug. #id:line.Day6.028
\Lavinia does not look up from the sofa cushion as the ceiling light goes out. #id:line.Day6.029
\“The glare?” she asks. #id:line.Day6.030
\“It reflects in the screen.” #id:line.Day6.031
\“It never bothered you before.” #id:line.Day6.032
\Rain streaking the dark window pane above the sofa. #id:line.Day6.033
\On the rug beside the low table, a practice skirt still damp at the hem, her head resting back against the wool cushion under the white ceiling bulb. #id:line.Day6.034
\Laying the back of a cool wrist across her closed eyelids to block the glare; her eyelashes brushing once against the skin before the hand rises to click the wall switch down. #id:line.Day6.035
\From the corner of the sofa, Lavinia looks toward the television. #id:line.Day6.036
\“They've been in that corridor for ten minutes,” she says. #id:line.Day6.037
\“Nine,” I say. #id:line.Day6.038
\“That isn't better.” #id:line.Day6.039
\“No. He's opened the same door four times without once checking the latch first.” My fingers tap once against the rim of the saucer. “If he wanted to be inside the room, he'd have taken the key by now.” #id:line.Day6.040
\On the screen, the man in the wet raincoat opens a heavy mahogany door, steps through the frame, and finds himself walking down the exact same carpeted corridor from the opposite end. #id:line.Day6.041
\“We could stop it,” she says. #id:line.Day6.042
\“I'd like to see whether he ever reaches a room.” #id:line.Day6.043
\Lavinia settles back against the cushion. The pale blue light from the television moves across the hollow of her throat and the corner of her mouth. #id:line.Day6.044
\Without turning her head toward my chair, she asks\: “What did he just take out of his pocket?” #id:line.Day6.045
\Silence for a beat. #id:line.Day6.046
\“A key?” I say. #id:line.Day6.047
\“He didn't put his hand in his pocket.” Lavinia turns her head on the cushion and catches my eyes on the side of her face instead of the television. “Watch the film, not me.” #id:line.Day6.048
\Beside her saucer on the low table, her phone plays a slow piano recording at low volume. With her teacup resting against her knee, she hums a short four-note phrase—coming in half a beat before the piano reaches it, while our voices cover the middle bars of the track. #id:line.Day6.049
\“You're early,” I say. #id:line.Day6.050
\“Not on the other recording.” #id:line.Day6.051
\When the track changes, I look toward her phone beside the teapot. “What was the one before this? We talked through half of it.” #id:line.Day6.052
\“You were answering me.” #id:line.Day6.053
\“I won't this time.” #id:line.Day6.054
\Lavinia restarts the piano track—and before she settles back, I set my cup down on the saucer and tip my head toward the narrow strip of rug between the low table and the television. #id:line.Day6.055
\“Shall we?” #id:line.Day6.056
\Lavinia looks at my offered hand, then at the phone. #id:line.Day6.057
\“Not to this. Wait.” #id:line.Day6.058
\She picks up her phone from beside the teapot, scrolls through two lines, and starts the other recording. After listening to its opening bars, she sets the phone down on the wood. #id:line.Day6.059
\“This one.” #id:line.Day6.060
\I stand and offer my hand. #id:line.Day6.061
\Lavinia takes it. When we step into the narrow space beside the table, she lays her left hand on my shoulder; I step closer and rest my right palm at the small of her back. #id:line.Day6.062
\As we begin, my gaze drops once toward the edge of the rug. #id:line.Day6.063
\Lavinia immediately makes her next step smaller. #id:line.Day6.064
\“You're still watching your feet,” she says softly. #id:line.Day6.065
\“This is an improvement.” #id:line.Day6.066
\She laughs. #id:line.Day6.067
\We do not move far. Lavinia begins a slow turn and feels me follow; on the next bar, I change direction first, and she adjusts her weight without saying anything. The low table passes out of her line of sight, then returns. #id:line.Day6.068
\When the song finishes, I glance toward the phone on the table. “Another?” #id:line.Day6.069
\“Not yet.” #id:line.Day6.070
\Lavinia keeps her hand in mine. Without any music in the room—only the faint hum of the television and the man on screen opening another door onto the same hallway—she takes one more slow step across the rug, and I move with her for three silent turns. #id:line.Day6.071
\Finally she stops. #id:line.Day6.072
\“I want my tea,” she murmurs. #id:line.Day6.073
\My hand opens from hers, and we return to our seats. #id:line.Day6.074
\Lavinia picks up her cup and takes a sip of the stronger tea. #id:line.Day6.075
\“In the story we read before I went,” she says quietly over the rim, “you said the woman at the end was right to send her away.” #id:line.Day6.076
\“I changed my mind the next day,” I say. #id:line.Day6.077
\“You wrote it down.” #id:line.Day6.078
\Her hand reaches to the low shelf beside the sofa and lifts a dark-green linen notebook—but instead of opening it, she holds it closed against her chest, her fingers pressing into the linen cover. #id:line.Day6.079
\“May I see it?” I ask. #id:line.Day6.080
\Lavinia does not open the cover. In the blue light from the television, her eyes stay on mine—quieter now, without the teasing edge. #id:line.Day6.081
\“Say it first,” she whispers. #id:line.Day6.082
\“You already have the note in your hand.” #id:line.Day6.083
\“I know what the paper says. I've had the paper for months.” Her fingers tighten slightly on the green cover. “I want to hear you say it before you see it.” #id:line.Day6.084
\On the screen, the corridor door swings shut in silence. #id:line.Day6.085
\“You were right about the ending,” I say softly. “She shouldn't have let her go.” #id:line.Day6.086
\For a second Lavinia's throat moves as she swallows. Then she opens the green cover to a marked page and passes it across the low table into my hands. #id:line.Day6.087
\Fixed to the upper margin of the page with a strip of yellowed tape is a small square of paper in my handwriting\: #id:line.Day6.088
\You were right about the ending. #id:line.Day6.089
\“Just that page,” she murmurs. #id:line.Day6.090
\“I still disliked the last chapter.” #id:line.Day6.091
\“You didn't put that in the note.” #id:line.Day6.092
\When the next track on her phone ends in a soft click, Lavinia turns half-sideways on the sofa to look through her recordings. #id:line.Day6.093
\The green notebook lies open across my knees. #id:line.Day6.094
\Beneath the bottom edge of my taped note, the lower half of the page is covered in Lavinia's slanted dark ink—two lines in Romanian at the top, and below them an exchange in English laid out like a play script. #id:line.Day6.095
\In the second line, she had written the milder word leave first, struck it through twice with a heavy nib, and rewritten it harder above the line\: #id:line.Day6.096
\P\: I'd do it again if you limped. #id:line.Day6.097
\L\: Even if I told you to l̶e̶a̶v̶e̶ get out? #id:line.Day6.098
\P\: Don't tell me to get out when you've already taken my coat. #id:line.Day6.099
\And at the very bottom margin of the page, underlined twice in the same dark ink\: #id:line.Day6.100
\Don't ask her to stay. #id:line.Day6.101
\Lavinia looks back from her phone before starting the track. #id:line.Day6.102
\Her eyes drop immediately to my thumb holding the lower corner of the taped note up from the page. #id:line.Day6.103
\“Not that page,” she says, extending her hand palm-up between us. #id:line.Day6.104
\The notebook stays open across my knees. #id:line.Day6.105
\“You wrote my part,” I say quietly. #id:line.Day6.106
\“Give it back.” #id:line.Day6.107
\“Do I sound like that to you?” #id:line.Day6.108
\Slowly, Lavinia lowers her extended hand to her knee. In the blue flicker from the television—where the man in the raincoat is opening another identical door—her eyes sharpen. #id:line.Day6.109
\“A minute ago you had to say it before you saw it,” she says, her English suddenly crisp and stripped of play. “Now that you've seen this one without asking, let's hear you say it aloud.” #id:line.Day6.110
\“Lavinia—” #id:line.Day6.111
\“Go on. The first line. Let's hear whether I got your voice right.” #id:line.Day6.112
\My eyes drop to the slanted ink on the page. #id:line.Day6.113
\“I would be concerned if the pain returned.” #id:line.Day6.114
\“Stop.” Lavinia leans forward on the sofa, her palm striking the wool cushion beside her hip. “That isn't what is written on the page. Don't polish it. You usually only amend my documents when I'm in another room.” #id:line.Day6.115
\“Read what is written, Priscilla,” she says softly, her eyes gleaming in the blue light. “From the top. Without improving me.” #id:line.Day6.116
# self-talk
\Stand up and read the next line right between her knees. #id:line.Day6.117
label Day6.thought
return

label Day6.rehearsal.yes
\My chair slides back an inch on the rug. Two unhurried paces close the gap between the low table and the sofa until my shins brush the hem of her skirt right between her knees. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.001
\Lavinia does not lean back, though she has to tilt her chin up to keep my face in view. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.002
\“I'd do it again if you limped,” I read, low and level. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.003
\“Still too polite,” she whispers, though her fingers tighten on the edge of the sofa cushion. “You were much more ruthless when you altered the form before I left. Read the next one.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.004
\I let the open notebook close against my thigh, no longer looking at the ink, and brace my free hand on the back cushion of the sofa an inch from her bare collarbone. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.005
\“Don't tell me to get out when you've already taken my coat.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.006
\Lavinia's hand rises and closes hard around my wrist braced beside her neck—her nails pressing into the pulse point, holding my arm against her collarbone instead of pushing it away. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.007
\When her hand loosens around my wrist, I give the closed notebook back to her. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.yes.008
label Day6.rehearsal.yes.next
return

label Day6.rehearsal.default
\From the chair across the low table, my gaze stays on her mouth instead of the paper. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.001
\“I'd do it again if you limped.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.002
\“A little flat,” Lavinia says, resting her chin on her knuckles. “You were much more convincing before I went away. Read the next one.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.003
\“Don't tell me to get out when you've already taken my coat.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.004
\Lavinia reaches across the low table, draws the green notebook from between my fingers, and closes it across her lap. #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.005
\“You see?” she says softly. “You didn't need to edit a single word.” #id:line.Day6.rehearsal.default.006
label Day6.rehearsal.default.next
return

label Day6.impulse
\On the television screen, the corridor door clicks shut without a word of dialogue. #id:line.Day6.impulse.001
\Lavinia holds the closed green notebook against her lap. #id:line.Day6.impulse.002
\“That line at the bottom wasn't written for you,” she says. #id:line.Day6.impulse.003
\“I know.” #id:line.Day6.impulse.004
\“You don't know when I wrote it.” #id:line.Day6.impulse.005
\“No.” #id:line.Day6.impulse.006
\Take the line at the bottom off the page and stay the night. #id:line.Day6.impulse.007
label Day6.impulse.playable
label Day6.impulse.end
return

label Day6.A
\“I'm asking about tonight,” I say quietly, not reaching for the notebook. “I'd like to stay longer.” #id:line.Day6.A.001
\Lavinia looks at me for a long beat, then sets the green notebook on the shelf behind her head. #id:line.Day6.A.002
\“Then stay.” #id:line.Day6.A.003
\I set my cup down on the saucer. Lavinia picks up her phone and looks at the first piano track. #id:line.Day6.A.004
\“I preferred the first time we heard that song,” she says. #id:line.Day6.A.005
\“We talked through part of it.” #id:line.Day6.A.006
\“I know. I preferred it that way.” #id:line.Day6.A.007
\As I begin to ask whether the man on the television has reached the stairs yet, Lavinia returns the earlier track not to its beginning, but directly to the middle passage our voices covered before—letting my sentence and her answer cover the exact same bars of music a second time. #id:line.Day6.A.008
label Day6.A.next
return

label Day6.B
\My fingers touch the linen corner of the notebook on her lap, then draw back. #id:line.Day6.B.001
\“You have morning call tomorrow. I shouldn't keep you up if you want the room to yourself.” #id:line.Day6.B.002
\Lavinia watches me from the corner of the sofa. #id:line.Day6.B.003
\“You hide her mug above my refrigerator, read my private page, and now you're offering to go home?” #id:line.Day6.B.004
\“Unless you ask me to stay.” #id:line.Day6.B.005
\“I invited you.” #id:line.Day6.B.006
\Neither of us moves closer; the stronger tea cools in the two porcelain cups while the corridor tape runs on. #id:line.Day6.B.007
label Day6.B.next
return

label Day6.C
\“You wrote both parts,” I say quietly. “Did you write what happens after she says that?” #id:line.Day6.C.001
\“No. The page ended.” #id:line.Day6.C.002
\“May I sit with you first?” #id:line.Day6.C.003
\“Before what?” #id:line.Day6.C.004
\“Before you decide whether to tell me to get out.” #id:line.Day6.C.005
\Lavinia looks down at the cushion beside her thigh, then lifts her phone and the green notebook onto the shelf to clear the wool. #id:line.Day6.C.006
\“Sit down,” she says. “And watch the film, not me.” #id:line.Day6.C.007
\On the sofa, less than an inch separates our shoulders. While I point out that the man on the screen is wearing a different raincoat in the next doorway, Lavinia reaches for her phone and returns the first piano track directly to the middle passage our voices covered earlier—letting our ongoing conversation cover the music again as her shoulder settles against mine. #id:line.Day6.C.008
label Day6.C.next
return

label Day6.D
\One hand reaches down, takes the closed green notebook from her lap, and opens it back to the marked page. #id:line.Day6.D.001
\Before Lavinia can catch my wrist, two fingers pinch the bottom margin—where Don't ask her to stay is underlined twice—and tear the narrow strip of paper cleanly along the crease. #id:line.Day6.D.002
\Lavinia's breath catches in her throat. #id:line.Day6.D.003
\“What are you doing?” #id:line.Day6.D.004
\The torn strip of paper folds once and slips into my pocket. The notebook goes onto the low table. #id:line.Day6.D.005
\“Amending the ending,” I say softly. “You left a contradiction at the bottom.” #id:line.Day6.D.006
\“Get out.” #id:line.Day6.D.007
\“Tell me without holding my sleeve.” #id:line.Day6.D.008
\In the blue flicker of the television screen, Lavinia looks down\: her left hand has caught the cuff of my shirt and is twisting the linen tight around my wrist. #id:line.Day6.D.009
\She does not let go. Instead, she pulls my cuff hard enough to bring my knee down onto the sofa cushion right between her hip and the armrest. #id:line.Day6.D.010
\“You're insufferable,” she whispers against my mouth, her free hand tangling into the hair behind my ear and drawing my head down until our foreheads strike lightly together. “You think everything in this flat belongs to you.” #id:line.Day6.D.011
# self-talk
\“Only the cup with the chip,” I murmur, and slide my right arm around her waist—my palm settling flat over the thin wool at her ribs—until her back presses into the sofa cushions beneath me. #id:line.Day6.D.012
label Day6.D.thought
return

label Day6.D.default
\Beneath me on the cushion, Lavinia's fingers uncurl from my twisted cuff only to slide up inside my sleeve, holding my forearm against her ribs while her mouth parts against mine—warm and bitter-sweet with the stronger tea. #id:line.Day6.D.default.001
\“I told you to watch the film,” she whispers against my lips without opening her eyes. #id:line.Day6.D.default.002
\“I'm watching,” I murmur against her throat. #id:line.Day6.D.default.003
\On the television screen behind our shoulders, the man in the raincoat opens another door onto the exact same corridor. Neither of us turns to look. #id:line.Day6.D.default.004
label Day6.D.default.next
return

label Day6.D.word
\“You wrote leave first,” I whisper against her lips, my thumb tracing the rib seam beneath her cardigan. “Why did you cross it out?” #id:line.Day6.D.word.001
\“Because you never listen to leave,” she breathes, pulling my mouth down to hers as the screen flickers blue across the rug. #id:line.Day6.D.word.002
label Day6.D.word.next
return

label Day6.D.dark
\Without lifting my weight from her waist, my left hand reaches blindly behind the sofa arm and presses the power switch on the television set. #id:line.Day6.D.dark.001
\The blue corridor collapses into a single white pinpoint and vanishes, leaving only the corner lamp and Lavinia's hands pulling me down by the collar. #id:line.Day6.D.dark.002
label Day6.D.dark.next
return

label Day6.E
\“You wrote both parts,” I say quietly. “Did you write what happens after she says that?” #id:line.Day6.E.001
\“No. The page ended.” #id:line.Day6.E.002
\“May I sit with you first?” #id:line.Day6.E.003
\“Before what?” #id:line.Day6.E.004
\“Before you decide whether to tell me to get out.” #id:line.Day6.E.005
\Lavinia looks down at the cushion beside her thigh, then lifts her phone and the green notebook onto the shelf to clear the wool. #id:line.Day6.E.006
\“Sit down,” she says. “And watch the film, not me.” #id:line.Day6.E.007
\On the sofa, less than an inch separates our shoulders. While I point out that the man on the screen is wearing a different raincoat in the next doorway, Lavinia reaches for her phone and returns the first piano track directly to the middle passage our voices covered earlier—letting our ongoing conversation cover the music again as her shoulder settles against mine. #id:line.Day6.E.008
\On the screen, the same brass door handle turns again. Same shadow across the carpet. Same white scratch on the lower left frame of the film. #id:line.Day6.E.009
\A tape does not repeat the exact same scratch fourteen times without a splice. #id:line.Day6.E.010
\And when the cupboard door opened in the kitchen, the chipped porcelain cup was already sitting at the very front edge of the shelf—next to a honey jar whose lid had already been loosened. #id:line.Day6.E.011
\You. #id:line.Day6.E.012
\You keep opening the same corridor until the two of us walk into the room you want. #id:line.Day6.E.013
\Lavinia's shoulder rises and falls warmly against my chest. #id:line.Day6.E.014
\Let the tape run all night if you like. The door is already shut behind us. #id:line.Day6.E.015
label Day6.E.next
return

